WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:21.960
Idea. At the doctor's you may fill in a form with tick boxes. Or the doctor may just ask you questions and talk. Researchers do the same. A questionnaire is a written set of questions. An interview is where the researcher asks the questions face to face or by phone. Both are self-report methods, because people report on themselves.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:39.660
Closed-ended questions have fixed answer options, such as tick boxes or a rating scale. They give quantitative data, which is numbers. Open-ended questions let people answer in their own words. They give qualitative data, which is descriptions in words.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:59.459
Interviews come in three kinds. A structured interview uses the same preset questions in the same order for everyone. A semi-structured interview has some set questions and freedom to ask follow-ups. An unstructured interview is more like a conversation about a topic, with no fixed questions.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:10.301
Self-report has weaknesses. People may give answers that make them look good. This is social desirability bias. They may also misunderstand a question.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:18.719
This type of question asks you to name, define or write a question or interview type, or explain why it suits a study.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:38.937
Method. Step one: decide which format or question type is described. Fixed options mean closed or structured. Free answers mean open or unstructured. Step two: link it to the data it gives, numbers or words. Step three: apply your point to the topic of the study.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:55.218
For a twelve-mark evaluation, describe the method, apply it to the named study, give strengths and weaknesses, then a justified conclusion. The trap in short questions is a closed question with no answer options. That is not closed.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:11.891
Worked example. Here is a real exam question. Jason will use a questionnaire to see if people of different ages use social media differently. We must write one open-ended and one closed-ended question for it. It is worth two marks.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:21.849
Step one. The closed question needs fixed answer options. Keep it on the topic, social media use. Give how often as tick boxes.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:31.303
Step two. The open question lets people answer in their own words. Ask why, or how. For example, why do you use social media?

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:44.209
A tempting wrong route is to write the closed question with no options. How often do you use social media? Without tick boxes or a scale, it looks open. So always show the options.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:03:03.083
Now the exam-ready answer. Open: why do you use social media? Closed: how often do you use social media, with three tick boxes, under one hour, one to three hours, over three hours a day. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:15.387
Common mistake. A common mistake is calling open-ended a type of interview. Open and closed describe questions. Structured and unstructured describe interviews.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:33.307
Your turn. Now you try. Jamie used a questionnaire with open-ended and closed-ended questions about personality and obedience. Describe one reason why Jamie may have used open-ended questions. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:50.246
Answer. Open-ended questions let people answer in their own words. So Jamie would get detailed, qualitative data. For example, people could explain why they obeyed an authority figure. That gives a fuller picture of their personality.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:04:04.676
A common wrong answer says open-ended questions are quick and easy to compare. That describes closed questions. Open answers are different for each person, so they are harder to compare. That loses the mark.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.581
So find the format. Link it to its data. Apply it to the topic.
